(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What should a patient with a pyogenic granuloma be told?","choices":["It is benign and often resolves over months to years; treatment is available if wanted","The lesion is premalignant and must be excised urgently to prevent transformation","The lesion is infectious and household contacts should be examined","The lesion will fade within a year and no follow-up is necessary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatments exist for cosmetic reasons or to prevent bleeding. If it recurs, early follow-up is appropriate, because small lesions are easier to treat than large ones. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has a sharply circumscribed jet-black papule 5 mm across on the thigh, with faint blue-grey shading at one edge. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Spitz naevus of the lower extremity","A dysplastic naevus of the lower extremity","Pigmented spindle cell naevus, also called Reed naevus","A blue naevus of the lower extremity or the thigh"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — commonest in the thirties, females more than males, on the lower extremities and mainly the thigh. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman handling her pet tarantula developed a red painful eye an hour later and now has blurred vision. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent ophthalmology referral, since embedded hairs can cause corneal granuloma","Topical corticosteroid to the eyelid skin with review in one week's time","Oral antihistamine with artificial tears and reassessment in three days","Irrigation of the eye at home with saline and no further follow-up needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical corticosteroid addresses only the cutaneous reaction. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 30-year-old has extensive urushiol contact dermatitis covering both arms, the neck and part of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A five-day course of oral prednisone without any taper","Oral antihistamines alone","High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks","High-potency topical corticosteroids alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — extensive disease needs systemic therapy, and early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has been treated for head lice and her mother asks what to do about the house. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Have the home professionally fumigated and then discard all the pillows and the soft furnishings","Wash everything at 60 degrees Celsius and treat every household member immediately","Engage a professional exterminator to treat cracks and crevices around the beds","Put clothing and bedding from the past week in the dryer or bag it for two weeks, wash combs and vacuum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fumigation is specifically not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl with cercarial dermatitis has pain and swelling with the itch, worst about two days after exposure. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Overnight topical permethrin applied to the whole skin surface with a repeat dose at one week","Oral doxycycline for ten days to cover the organism carried in the fresh water","Antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain and topical or oral glucocorticoids","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days to clear the parasite from the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the pain and swelling peak at 48 to 72 hours before settling. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has erythema nodosum, bilateral ankle arthritis and hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph. Which is the most likely underlying diagnosis?","choices":["Primary tuberculosis with mediastinal involvement","Streptococcal infection with post-infectious reaction","Sarcoidosis presenting as Löfgren syndrome","Inflammatory bowel disease, most likely Crohn's disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it has the best prognosis of all sarcoidosis presentations, with over 90% spontaneous remission. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has more than twenty rough scaly papules spread across her forehead, cheeks and nose. Individually they are small and none is indurated. Which treatment approach fits best?","choices":["Observation alone, since none of the lesions is currently indurated or bleeding","Field-directed therapy such as topical fluorouracil, imiquimod or photodynamic therapy","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy to each lesion in turn at this visit","Excision of each lesion with a two-millimetre margin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — multiple lesions across one anatomic region is field cancerization, which is treated as a field. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is a positive Nikolsky sign?","choices":["Lesions develop at sites of trauma","Fine white lacy lines appearing across the surface of the affected plaques","The top layers of skin slip away or slough off from the lower layers when rubbed","Removal of scale produces pinpoint bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — positive in pemphigus, negative in bullous pemphigoid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of lentigines and where they occur.","choices":["Small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across, appearing on sun-exposed skin in fair individuals","Beige to brown to black papules that feel velvety and look stuck onto the surface of the skin","Well-circumscribed round to oval uniformly black or brown macules under 5 mm, on skin, conjunctiva and mucocutaneous surfaces","Well-defined macules with irregular borders that coalesce at sites of severe sunburn, from under 1 mm to several centimetres"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient with folliculitis has not responded to a topical antibiotic and the infection is now extensive. Which oral agents apply?","choices":["Erythromycin or clarithromycin","Doxycycline or minocycline","Cephalexin or dicloxacillin","Penicillin V or amoxicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or linezolid if resistance is suspected. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with cutaneous lichen planus on the wrists has not improved with emollients. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Superpotent topical steroids","Topical tacrolimus ointment","Oral prednisone","PUVA phototherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical tacrolimus is used for oral and vaginal disease, oral steroids for severe cases, and phototherapy for refractory disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 178"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman has had several weeks of intense itching with urticarial plaques but no blisters. She is otherwise well. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Early pemphigus vulgaris","Lichen simplex chronicus","The prodromal phase of bullous pemphigoid, which may precede bullae by weeks to months","Chronic idiopathic urticaria entirely unrelated to any blistering disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the prodrome presents as pruritic urticarial or oedematous lesions before the blistering phase. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which pairing correctly separates pretest from posttest probability?","choices":["Pretest is estimated from signs, symptoms, history and frequency; posttest additionally reflects the test result","Pretest probability applies to screening programmes only, while posttest probability applies to diagnostic testing","Pretest uses sensitivity and specificity; posttest uses risk factors","Both are determined solely by how common the disease is"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This matches the lecture's two definitions and their listed inputs. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has thickened discoloured toenails and you decide to confirm the diagnosis before prescribing. Which tests does the lecture list, and how should the sample be taken?","choices":["A Wood lamp examination followed by biopsy of the nail matrix; sample only after six weeks of topical therapy has failed","Potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid–Schiff stain of clippings, culture, or amplification testing; sample the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed","Potassium hydroxide microscopy or fungal culture only; sample the most distal free edge of the nail plate before any trimming of the onycholytic nail is performed","Serology for dermatophyte antibodies or a blood culture; no local sampling of the nail is required at any stage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — sample subungual debris after trimming the onycholytic nail. Sampling proximally is the part most often missed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man of Mediterranean origin has slowly progressive purple plaques on both lower legs over several years. He is not immunosuppressed. Which form of Kaposi sarcoma is this?","choices":["Iatrogenic — occurring with immunosuppressive therapy and improving when doses are reduced","Epidemic — occurring in acquired immunodeficiency and treated first with antiretroviral therapy","Classic — older men, chronic course, rarely fatal, related to age-associated immune senescence","Endemic — often aggressive in young Black men in equatorial Africa and sometimes rapidly fatal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the chronic indolent form. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with dermatosis papulosa nigrans asks about removing the lesions. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Wide excision is needed because these lesions can transform into melanoma over time","They are best left untreated; excision, curettage or laser can be used, but cryotherapy is avoided","Cryotherapy at the first visit is the treatment of choice, with laser reserved for recurrences","Topical depigmenting agents should be tried first, since the lesions are pigmented macules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy risks post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in this group. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which rosacea complication requires urgent ophthalmology referral?","choices":["Papules and pustules spreading beyond the central face","Ocular rosacea with keratitis, visual symptoms or corneal involvement","Rhinophyma causing nasal airway narrowing over several years","Persistent central facial erythema unresponsive to topical therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — untreated ocular disease risks corneal scarring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How does a callus differ from a corn?","choices":["It comes from repeated scratching, is lichenified with exaggerated skin markings, is plaque-like, and itches intensely","It comes from broad-area pressure, is diffusely thickened without a central core, is larger and irregular, and is usually painless","It comes from focal pressure, has a cone-shaped keratin core, is smaller and well defined, and hurts on direct pressure","It comes from viral infection, has a blackened centre, interrupts the skin lines, and hurts on side pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — no core, bigger, vaguer, and it does not hurt. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why must malignancy and infection be excluded in a rapidly expanding ulcer before treating it as pyoderma gangrenosum?","choices":["Because the diagnosis is one of exclusion and the immunosuppression used would worsen an infection","Because pyoderma gangrenosum is itself a premalignant condition","Because antibiotics would become the first-line treatment once an infection is confirmed","Because a biopsy cannot be taken once immunosuppression has started"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the question to answer is why this ulcer is suddenly expanding. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A colleague suggests incising and draining the swollen fingertip of a patient with herpetic whitlow. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Do not incise and drain it, because that does not treat the virus and can delay healing","Incise and drain it, because decompression shortens the course of the infection","Incise and drain it, but only after 72 hours of oral antiviral therapy have failed","Do not incise and drain it, because the lesion is sterile and will resolve within 24 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — explicitly contraindicated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"What causes a pressure injury?","choices":["Unrelieved pressure damaging underlying tissue, generally soft tissue compressed between a bony prominence and an external surface for a prolonged time","Repeated friction over a broad area of skin, producing diffuse hyperkeratosis with a poorly defined margin and no central keratin core at all","Disruption of the skin over the coccyx drawing in hair and debris, with follicular plugging that prevents drainage","Loss of the control mechanisms that normally regulate tissue repair, producing overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the injury ranges from intact skin to ulcers reaching bone. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 72-year-old outdoor worker has rough, scaly, erythematous papules with a sandpaper texture scattered across his balding scalp and dorsal hands. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Bowen disease","Scalp psoriasis","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sandpaper texture on chronically sun-exposed skin, the commonest precancerous lesion worldwide. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"How does an acute pilonidal abscess present, and how does chronic disease present?","choices":["Acutely, non-blanchable erythema over intact skin; chronically, a deep ulcer extending as far as bone","Acutely, sudden pain and swelling in or along the gluteal cleft; chronically, recurrent drainage and pain from one or more sinus tracts","Acutely, recurrent purulent drainage from several sinus tracts; chronically, sudden pain and swelling over the sacrum","Acutely, a rapidly growing vascular nodule that bleeds easily; chronically, a flat uniformly pigmented patch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a hair may occasionally be seen protruding from a sinus opening. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 62-year-old has a biopsy-confirmed superficial basal cell carcinoma on his upper back, with no high-risk features. He would prefer to avoid surgery. Which option is appropriate, and what must follow it?","choices":["Fluorouracil twice daily for two weeks, with clinical clearance confirmed afterwards","Vismodegib, since it avoids surgery altogether","Imiquimod five nights weekly for six to ten weeks, with clinical clearance confirmed afterwards","Imiquimod five nights weekly for six to ten weeks, with no further review needed if the lesion disappears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is acceptable for selected superficial disease, but clearance must be verified. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man's biopsy reports squamous cell carcinoma in situ on the forearm with no high-risk features. Which options does the deck give?","choices":["Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery only","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab only","Hedgehog pathway inhibitors, vismodegib or sonidegib","Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three options for appropriately selected in situ disease. Or curettage and electrodesiccation. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which three lice cause pediculosis, and which body regions do they affect?","choices":["Phthirus pubis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus capitis on pubic skin, and Cimex pilosellus on the trunk","Pediculus humanus capitis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus humanus on the trunk, Phthirus pubis on pubic skin","Pediculus humanus capitis on the trunk, Pediculus humanus humanus on the scalp, Phthirus pubis on the eyelashes","Sarcoptes scabiei on the scalp, Cimex lectularius on the trunk, and Phthirus pubis on pubic skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The body louse is about 30% larger than the head louse. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 23-year-old has finely scaling tan macules on the trunk, and you perform potassium hydroxide microscopy on a scale from one lesion. Which finding would you expect?","choices":["Long branching septate hyphae without any yeast forms present","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae without any short hyphal fragments","Multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions","Short hyphae with clusters of yeast, described as \"spaghetti and meatballs\""],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is the classic appearance. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 10-year-old boy's parents want to avoid chemical treatment for head lice. Which physical approach can be offered, and with what caveat?","choices":["Waiting without treatment, since head lice clear spontaneously within a few weeks","Combing nits out after two minutes of hair moisturiser every few days, though it is slow and needs adjuvant therapy","Applying mayonnaise or mineral oil weekly to smother the live lice and their attached eggs on the hair","Washing the hair daily with an antibacterial shampoo until no live lice are seen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — head shaving is the other physical option named. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which comorbidities are patients with psoriasis at increased risk of?","choices":["Asthma and allergic rhinitis","Cardiovascular events, type 2 diabetes mellitus, metabolic syndrome and lymphoma","Chronic kidney disease, hepatic cirrhosis and early-onset osteoporosis","Osteoporosis and vitamin D deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is a systemic inflammatory disease, not only a skin condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 155"},{"q":"Which single feature most usefully distinguishes erythema nodosum from nodular vasculitis?","choices":["Erythema nodosum nodules occur exclusively in men","Erythema nodosum nodules do not ulcerate","Erythema nodosum nodules appear only on the posterior legs","Erythema nodosum nodules are painless on palpation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they sit on the anterior shins whereas nodular vasculitis favours the posterior legs and ulcerates. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What are the two primary clinical priorities in stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Distinguishing it from cellulitis, and determining whether compression therapy can be used safely","Identifying the causative organism by culture and selecting an appropriate antibiotic","Determining the allergen and arranging patch testing","Staging the ulcer and measuring its depth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both are named as the priorities. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 70-year-old has a biopsy-proven invasive squamous cell carcinoma 1.4 cm across on the temple. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Imiquimod or topical fluorouracil, since topical therapy is appropriate for invasive disease at this size","Programmed death 1 blockade, since a lesion over 1 cm is by definition advanced disease","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy, since the temple tolerates destruction well cosmetically","Mohs micrographic surgery, since the temple is a high-risk site and the lesion exceeds 1 cm on the face"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — two separate Mohs indications are met. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which shaving advice is given for pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical antibiotic","Stop shaving if possible, use clean razors, and avoid lift-and-cut razor systems","Shave daily against the grain with a multi-blade razor to cut hairs below the surface","Shave weekly with an electric razor set to the closest possible cutting length"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mild razor angles and single or at most double blades are preferred. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which two combination topical products are named for reducing antibiotic resistance in acne?","choices":["Differin (adapalene with benzoyl peroxide) and Tazorac (tazarotene with benzoyl peroxide)","Cleocin T (clindamycin with salicylic acid) and Erygel (erythromycin with salicylic acid)","Altabax (retapamulin with mupirocin) and Bactroban (mupirocin with benzoyl peroxide)","BenzaClin (clindamycin with benzoyl peroxide) and Benzamycin (erythromycin with benzoyl peroxide)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both pair a topical antibiotic with benzoyl peroxide. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which diagnostic testing does this patient's pilonidal disease require?","choices":["Punch biopsy of the sinus opening","Wound culture before any intervention","None; the diagnosis is clinical","Magnetic resonance imaging to map any sinus tracts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — no testing is usually needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 41-year-old describes severe stabbing pain under one fingernail, worse in cold water, with an exquisitely tender point. The nail itself looks almost normal, with a small red-blue spot beneath it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subungual haematoma","Glomus tumour","Nail unit melanoma","Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the triad of paroxysmal pain, point tenderness and cold sensitivity. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Conditional probability, as introduced in this lecture, refers to which of the following?","choices":["The probability that a treatment will be tolerated by the patient","The probability that two clinicians reach the same diagnosis","The proportion of a population that will develop a disease over a lifetime","The probability of disease if another event, test result, or condition is present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines conditional probability as the probability of disease or event given that another event, test result, or condition is present. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which pathway does the MCR-1 receptor act through?","choices":["T-cell mediated cytotoxicity against melanocytes","Cyclic adenosine monophosphate, activating melanogenesis","The insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor pathway","Matrix metalloproteinase activation in the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — reduced activity promotes pheomelanin instead. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"You are not certain whether one of those lesions is a basal cell carcinoma. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start light electrocautery to all lesions and review","Dermoscopy, which can distinguish the two, with biopsy if concern remains","Excise all the lesions with clear margins","Reassure, since sebaceous hyperplasia never resembles a carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — patients often present worried about exactly this. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which variant of granuloma annulare carries the strongest systemic associations?","choices":["Generalised or disseminated, associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, dyslipidaemia and lymphoma","Localised disease, which predominates at about 75% of cases and affects children and young adults","Subcutaneous, occurring almost exclusively in children under six","Perforating, with central umbilication and extrusion of collagen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is more common in adults over forty. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does vitiligo differ from pityriasis versicolor on the deck's differential?","choices":["Vitiligo has an erythematous yellowish tint with soft greasy scales","Vitiligo begins with a herald patch and spreads in a Christmas tree pattern","Vitiligo shows short hyphae with yeast clusters on microscopy","Vitiligo is completely white — depigmented — and is an autoimmune disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pityriasis versicolor is hypopigmented rather than depigmented, and it scales. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which lesion is dermatosis papulosa nigrans described as identical to?","choices":["Small seborrheic keratoses","Small solar lentigines","Small blue naevi of the face","Small dysplastic naevi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the distinction is in the epidemiology and the distribution on face and neck. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What must be protected in Ramsay Hunt syndrome, and why?","choices":["The cornea, if eyelid closure is impaired","The tympanic membrane, if vesicles involve the canal","The inner ear, by avoiding all antivirals","The facial nerve, by avoiding corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the facial palsy can prevent the eye from closing. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman in Missouri was bitten while cleaning an attic. The site now shows a dark purple centre surrounded by a pale blanched ring, and beyond that a large irregular area of redness. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A hobo spider bite","Early cellulitis at the site","A brown recluse spider bite","A black widow spider bite"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the red, white and blue sign is the hallmark of this envenomation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How does the allylamine class of antifungals work, and how do you recognise one by name?","choices":["It blocks synthesis of ergosterol; the names end in -azole","It inhibits mitosis in dividing fungal cells; the names end in -fulvin","It blocks cell wall glucan synthesis; the names end in -fungin","It destroys the fungal cell membrane; the names end in -fine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — terbinafine and naftifine. Destroying the membrane prevents growth and ultimately kills the fungus. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"An 84-year-old nursing home resident has a reddened area over her sacrum. The skin is intact and the erythema does not blanch when you press on it. Which stage of pressure injury is this?","choices":["Deep tissue pressure injury","Unstageable","Stage 1","Stage 2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — non-blanchable erythema of intact skin. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In the FRAMES counselling strategy, what does the letter M represent?","choices":["Motivation of the clinician","Medication review","Monitoring of behaviour change","Menu of options"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FRAMES is feedback about personal risk, responsibility of the patient, advice to change, menu of options, empathetic style, and promote self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is neurocutaneous melanosis and which patients are at risk?","choices":["A related condition in patients with naevi on the head, neck or posterior midline, causing seizures, hydrocephalus, neurological deficits and vomiting","A related condition in patients with naevi on the trunk and extremities, causing pigmented macules within a tan background patch","A related condition in patients with over one hundred naevi by adolescence, carrying a high risk of progression to melanoma","A related condition in patients with multiple Spitz naevi, associated with an underlying familial cancer syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the prognosis is poor once neurological symptoms appear, usually in the first few years of life. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a single intensely itchy, well-demarcated thickened plaque on her lateral ankle with exaggerated skin markings and pigment change. She admits to scratching it most evenings while watching television. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Excise the plaque","Begin oral antibiotics","Begin an oral antifungal such as terbinafine, on the basis that the thickened scaly plaque is most likely a fungal infection of long standing","Break the itch-scratch cycle through education, emollients, behavioural substitution and nail care, with a limited course of a potent topical steroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — recurrence is common unless the initiating itch is controlled. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has hot tub folliculitis over the trunk. He is systemically well and the rash began four days ago. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral ciprofloxacin for a ten-day course to eradicate the organism completely","Incision and drainage of the largest pustules with culture of the material","Topical mupirocin three times daily until every lesion has fully resolved","Reassurance with dilute acetic acid compresses, since most clear in two to ten days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 3 tablespoons of 5% vinegar in a pint of water, 20 minutes, two to four times daily. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which finding in a woman with persistent acne points toward polycystic ovarian syndrome?","choices":["Flushing with hot drinks and visible facial telangiectasias","Pustules pierced by a central hair over the thighs and trunk","Premenstrual flares of acne with an otherwise normal cycle","Hirsutism together with irregular or absent menses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That combination indicates androgen hypersecretion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the principal risk a patient must understand before endoscopic thoracic sympathectomy?","choices":["Compensatory sweating elsewhere, occurring in over 30%","The need for retreatment every three to six months","Dry mouth and urinary retention as ongoing side effects","Permanent destruction of axillary glands by microwave energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the procedure is permanent, so the trade-off has to be discussed thoroughly beforehand. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Professor Jaquith treats a small-surface-area non-purulent cellulitis as an outpatient. What follow-up does she insist on, and why?","choices":["Return in one week, once the course is complete","No routine follow-up unless the patient deteriorates","Return in 72 hours for repeat blood cultures","Return in 24 to 48 hours, because it spreads so fast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — to confirm the antibiotic is working. That interval is the safety net on outpatient treatment. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:01:43"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with confirmed tungiasis of the foot asks how it will be treated. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Surgical excision or cryotherapy with tetanus prophylaxis and systemic antibiotics","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days with no procedural treatment needed","Overnight topical permethrin to the whole skin surface with a repeat dose at one week","Symptomatic care with antihistamines and oatmeal baths until the lesions resolve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dermoscopy showing ovoid eggs is what makes the diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 24-year-old with red hair and widespread freckling across her nose and cheeks asks what can be done about them. Which management does the deck support, and which must be avoided?","choices":["Surgical excision of the most prominent lesions","No treatment of any kind is available or appropriate","Sun protection, patient education and topical depigmenting agents; cryotherapy is NOT used","Cryotherapy to each individual lesion, with sun protection counselling afterwards"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy is difficult because of the size of the lesions, so intense pulsed light or lasers are preferred. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A patient has a single nail with longitudinal erythronychia, distal subungual hyperkeratosis and splinter haemorrhages. Which benign tumours does the deck associate with this pattern?","choices":["Glomus tumour or pyogenic granuloma","Digital myxoid cyst or subungual exostosis","Melanocytic naevus or lentigo","Onychopapilloma or onychomatricoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — single-nail longitudinal change with distal hyperkeratosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has molluscum lesions in the genital area. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Context-sensitive assessment, because location alone does not prove abuse","Immediate mandatory reporting on the basis of the lesion location alone","No assessment at all, because genital molluscum is expected in young children","Screening for sexually transmitted infections in every case as the sole action"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck is deliberate about this. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A test is described as able to correctly identify patients who do NOT have the disease. Which property is being described?","choices":["Pretest probability","Sensitivity","Specificity","Posttest probability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's paired prompt defines specificity as the ability to correctly identify patients who do not have the disease or condition. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"When should cultures be obtained in impetigo?","choices":["Only in children under three years of age, since they are the group at highest risk of complications","When the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, or when post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is present","In every case at first presentation, since staphylococcal and streptococcal disease are treated differently from one another","Only when the lesions have failed to respond after a full two-week course of topical mupirocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a health-care worker or teacher is the example given of high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"}]};
